  "author": "laozi",
  "attributionNote": "Traditional attribution to Laozi; the text is composite. Carus follows the received eighty-one-chapter edition and gives each chapter a descriptive title of his own ('Reason's Realization', 'Self-Culture'), which is how the translation is cited.",
  "description": "Paul Carus's English Daodejing, made with the young Teitaro Suzuki: eighty-one chapters in his own numbered paragraphs, each chapter headed with a title that states its theme. Carus reads the book as a universal philosophy of reason and says so plainly, translating Dao as 'Reason' and de as 'Virtue', and the result is a clear, deliberately modern rendering rather than a literal one. It was one of the first American translations of the whole text and remained the version many English readers met first.",
  "historicalContext": "Carus, the editor of the Open Court Publishing Company at La Salle, Illinois, published the translation in 1898 with the Chinese text facing the English; the edition printed here is the 1913 issue. Teitaro Suzuki, then working at Open Court, assisted with the Chinese. The book belongs to the same enterprise as the Suzuki–Carus translations of the Kan Ying P'ien and the Yin Chih Wen, which present Chinese religion to a Western readership through the categories of comparative religion.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete. All eighty-one chapters of Carus's English translation are streamed from the Internet Sacred Text Archive at read time, one printed chapter per chapter, with his numbered paragraphs indexed. The archive serves the Chinese text of the facing pages as images, so it is not part of this edition; Carus's introduction, his notes and the appendices printed with the volume are likewise not part of the work.",
